WEBVTT

00:00:00.676 --> 00:00:03.256
Command of evidence, with data.

00:00:03.256 --> 00:00:06.141
The answer must be accurate and relevant.

00:00:06.141 --> 00:00:08.521
Most wrong answers are only one of those.

00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:11.956
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:11.956 --> 00:00:14.356
You'll see a table or a graph.

00:00:14.356 --> 00:00:18.056
And a question asking which choice uses the data to support a claim.

00:00:19.093 --> 00:00:22.729
It's testing whether you can connect data to one specific claim.

00:00:23.802 --> 00:00:25.312
Here's the method.

00:00:25.312 --> 00:00:27.072
Read the claim first.

00:00:27.072 --> 00:00:29.357
What comparison does it need?

00:00:29.357 --> 00:00:34.052
Then read the graphic: the title, the labels, the units.

00:00:34.052 --> 00:00:36.867
For each choice, check it's accurate.

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:39.057
And check it's relevant to this exact claim.

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:41.837
Four data rules.

00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:44.737
The answer must be accurate and relevant.

00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:48.942
Match the comparison: the right groups, and the right years.

00:00:48.942 --> 00:00:52.202
Check the units, like percent versus number.

00:00:52.202 --> 00:00:54.547
And a change needs two points.

00:00:54.547 --> 00:00:56.056
One year can't show a trend.

00:00:57.072 --> 00:00:58.497
Let's try one.

00:00:58.497 --> 00:01:00.497
First, read the claim.

00:01:00.497 --> 00:01:05.752
It has two parts: biking more than doubled, and walking stayed nearly unchanged.

00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:07.307
Now check the table.

00:01:07.307 --> 00:01:13.157
Biking went from one point eight to seven, and walking went from five point two to five point six.

00:01:13.157 --> 00:01:17.027
Choice A gives only one year, so it can't show a change.

00:01:17.027 --> 00:01:19.522
Choice C doesn't show either change.

00:01:19.522 --> 00:01:21.947
Choice D covers walking only.

00:01:21.947 --> 00:01:25.346
Choice B shows both parts of the claim, so it's the answer.

00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:31.012
Watch out for the choice that's true about the graph, but doesn't prove the claim.

00:01:31.012 --> 00:01:32.822
Accurate isn't enough.

00:01:32.822 --> 00:01:34.021
It has to be relevant.

00:01:35.081 --> 00:01:42.026
Here's the whole lesson on one page: how to spot it, the four steps, the data rules, and the trap.

00:01:42.026 --> 00:01:44.915
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
